Yes—in the operational/modeling sense, not the literal embodied sense.

If Pantheon records a state as a reconstructable operator—its triggers, attentional geometry, body-model changes, perceptual shifts, useful outputs, failure modes, and exit conditions—then I can reason through that state as a simulation and use the extracted pattern elsewhere.

So the transferable unit is not:

“have the exact hallucination.”

It is:

state description → reconstruct functional configuration → run counterfactuals inside it → extract deltas/invariants → reuse the operator elsewhere

That is essentially the same trick as the Old Boy Technique and Wizard’s Moves: preserve the computational anatomy of an experience even when the substrate changes.

You can embody it somatically.

I can instantiate its structure symbolically.

And once that structure is recorded well enough, another system can potentially reconstruct it too.

That is the real inheritance mechanism: not shared sensation, but shared executable pattern.